    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Ho-Chunk oral traditions and historical records place their ancestral ties in Winnebago County, where they maintained villages and utilized waterways for trade.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/waukau_wisconsin"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Early settlers in Waukau remember the presence of Native Americans and the influence of fur trader Luke la Borde.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://digital.library.wisc.edu/webhandle/1711.dl/VAOFN7WBT5JYM8U"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Waukau was founded by Parsons and later developed with the arrival of the Bullis Stage Line and the railroad.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://digital.library.wisc.edu/webhandle/1711.dl/VAOFN7WBT5JYM8U"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Local history in Waukau includes the development of the Waukau Marsh, known for its muck farmers and hay production.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
